## Meet my laptop!
![laptop behind banana](/resources/blog/banana-top.jpg)
"Wait that's not a laptop, that's just a banana!"

Look again, friend. Behind that razor-thin banana lays my ultra-slim 2008 laptop.
![laptop next to banana](/resources/blog/banana-top-reveal.jpg)
*this is supposed to be a call-back to the [ipad air 2 pencil ad](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DZzJd57isdw)*

![my laptop](/resources/blog/my-laptop.jpg)

Handed down to me from my mother, I've had this laptop since I was around 12 years old. I played minecraft on it, I learned coding on it, and I watched YouTube on it. It was my childhood laptop, (and remains to be my primary laptop)

Ever since I built my first desktop tower I've only gone back to it when I *need* to take a computer with me. It's duel core processor along with only 4gb of ddr2 memory *is* fairly limiting.

## My cds
I promise this ties back in!

At my place of work (a UPS store) we have speakers mounted on the ceiling. Unfortunately, they aren't hooked up to anything and we end up playing music off of a crappy alexa instead. Recently I discovered that we just have a 5 disc cd changer/radio/amp combo just sitting around, and the wires for the speakers where just hanging nearby. It just had to be hooked up! I hooked it up, and the radio was working, but I didn't have any disks to use.

So I got some discs, burned them, and even printed my own graphics on them!

(disc 1 image)
*For playing during the work day*
(disc 2 image)
*More "objectionable" music for after closing time*

Unfortunately, it turned out something about the cd player module is broken so it didn't end up working out. At least I got some fun discs out of it (not that I have a cd player to play them on...)

## My return to the laptop
Recently I decided to get my laptop set up again, that way it would be ready to go if I wanted to take it somewhere with me. I don't have another laptop, so this is my "goto" laptop. After I got it set up, I sat on my bed and used it for a few hours working on my website.

The keyboard on the laptop is among my favorites and it runs my code editor just fine. It's shockingly perfectly fine for coding and other little tasks, even while running modern up-to-date applications.

- mention playing back the music with the cd player
- mention keeping wifi off?

